// The document, as a package.
//
// **The same forty-four names `slate:dom` exports, built on `external` instead of on thirteen
// hand-written builtins in the compiler's JavaScript back end.** A consumer changes one import line
// and nothing else:
//
//     import { byId, setText, on } from dom
//
// **Why it is a package at all.** Every name below is one JavaScript call away from what a browser
// already offers, and while they lived in the back end each of them cost a language release: a page
// that wanted `focus` waited for 0.0.37 and one that wanted `dispatch` waited again. `external` is
// the door the compiler grew so that this file could be ordinary slate, and a name added here ships
// the moment this package does.
//
// ## What a node is
//
// **A node IS the external -- the element itself, not an index into a table the engine keeps.** That
// is the one decision every other one here follows from, and it is a reversal of what the built-in
// module chose: `slate:dom` hands out an integer handle so that no foreign object ever becomes a
// slate value. `external` makes that trade unnecessary. An external is opaque, it cannot be printed
// as anything but `<external HTMLDivElement>`, it cannot be added to or iterated, and it matches no
// pattern but a bare name -- so a host object reaching a program is already the thing the handle
// table was protecting the program from.
//
// What it buys is that nothing has to be given back. A handle is a table slot with a lifetime; an
// element is a value the collector already knows how to forget. **`release` is exported and does
// nothing**, so a consumer written against the built-in module -- lath calls it in two places --
// compiles and runs unchanged.
//
// **`==` on two nodes is identity**, which is what `slate:dom` promised with integers and what
// `external` promises directly: two reads of one element compare equal because the host value
// behind them is one object.
//
// **AND A NODE CANNOT YET BE A KEY, WHICH IS THE ONE THING AN INTEGER HANDLE COULD DO AND THIS
// CANNOT.** A `Map`, a `Set` and an object all settle a key by hashing it and then comparing, and
// an external hashes by the wrapper slate built at the crossing rather than by the host value it
// holds -- so two reads of one element are `==` and hash apart, and a table keyed by a node loses
// the key it was just given. Every consumer of this module keys something by the node: lath holds
// what it last set on each one, and this module holds a listener's controller. **So a node as a key
// is what the language owes before this package can be finished**, and it is one line in the
// runtime -- the same rule `==` already follows, applied to the hash.
//
// ## What crosses the boundary
//
// Every function here is a property read, a property write or a method call on an external, and the
// marshalling is the language's rather than this file's: a whole `number` comes back an integer, a
// missing property comes back `null`, a string is a string, and anything else comes back external.
// **So the reading half of this module is nearly free** -- `tagName`, `nodeText` and `property` are
// one read each -- and the work is in the places where the host's answer is not the shape the spec
// says: an event, a mutation record, a location, a stored value.
//
// ## Under the interpreter
//
// **The declaration succeeds and only a host operation faults**, which is `external`'s own rule and
// costs this file nothing: an `external` declaration touches no host, so it is accepted where there
// is no host at all, and the first property read or call on the value it bound is what refuses. So
// the module imports on a server exactly as `slate:dom` does, and a program that asks for a document
// where there is none is told that the way it was run was the mistake rather than that the import
// was.
//
// **The sentence is not word for word `slate:dom`'s, and that difference is worth knowing.** The
// built-in module names the slate command -- `createElement` needs a document -- because it is
// written as a builtin and knows which one it is. What comes out here is the language's own refusal,
// naming the host operation and saying there is no JavaScript host: *calling `createElement` on an
// external reaches the JavaScript host, and the interpreter has no JavaScript host*. For most of
// these names the host's spelling IS the command -- `createElement`, `setAttribute`, `focus` -- and
// where it is not, `byId` reaching `getElementById`, the reader is still sent to the right place,
// which is the way the program was run.

// **ONE DECLARATION, AND EVERY HOST VALUE IS REACHED THROUGH IT.** `globalThis` is the one path
// every JavaScript host has, and that is the whole reason it is the only one named here.
//
// **A declaration is EAGER and TOTAL**: the path is walked where the declaration stands, and a path
// that names nothing is a fault quoting it. So `external document` at the top of this file would
// take the whole module with it on a host that has no document -- a program compiled with
// `slate js` and run under node, which is exactly where a server-rendering framework imports this
// and then guards its calls with `host()`. `slate:dom`'s rule is that **the module still exists and
// the import still succeeds**, because a refusal at the import is a complaint about the module and
// sends a reader looking for a spelling mistake in the one line that is right. A property read off
// `globalThis` answers `null` for a name that is not there, which is what keeps that rule true here.
//
// It is what keeps the interpreter's rule true as well. The declaration itself touches no host, so
// it is accepted where there is no host at all; the first property read is what faults, and it says
// there is no JavaScript host and which operation asked for one.
external globalThis

// The document, or `null` where there is none. **Asked per call rather than held in a `val`**, for
// the same reason the declaration is the only one: a top-level read is a host operation, and one
// standing at the top of this file would fault under the interpreter before a program had called
// anything.
page(command)
    throw "page has no body yet"

// The window, and `window.history` under it. `location` is read off the window rather than off
// `globalThis` so that a host installing a page under a `window` -- which is what a jsdom harness
// does -- is the same host a browser is.
frame(command)
    throw "frame has no body yet"

// The classes an event is built from, `globalThis.Event` and its two subclasses. **`dispatch`
// chooses between them by the event's name**, a `click` being a `MouseEvent` and a `keydown` a
// `KeyboardEvent`, because a handler reading `e.button` off a plain `Event` gets nothing and a
// router telling a left click from a middle one would let every dispatched click through to the
// browser.
eventClass(type)
    throw "eventClass has no body yet"

// **The two names a browser may genuinely lack, and neither is a fault at the import.**
// `EventSource` is absent from jsdom and from node, and `localStorage` is absent from a page a
// browser has been told to keep no data for -- so both are asked for where they are used and
// answered with the module's own sentence, which names what is missing rather than what a program
// was doing.
stream(command)
    throw "stream has no body yet"

held(command)
    throw "held has no body yet"

// -- making a node, and changing one --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **Each of these is one method call on `document` or on the node itself.** `createElement`,
// `createTextNode` and `createComment` answer host objects, which come back external and are
// therefore already nodes; `setAttribute` and the rest answer nothing and are called for what they
// do.
//
// **`value` and `checked` go through `setProperty` and never through `setAttribute`**, which is the
// pair a form turns on: the attribute says what the field started as and the property says what it
// holds now, so a re-render that set the attribute would leave a typed-in field alone and the page
// would appear frozen. This module does not enforce that -- both calls exist and a program picks --
// but every consumer of it depends on the distinction being real.

export createElement(tag)
    throw "createElement has no body yet"

export createText(s)
    throw "createText has no body yet"

export createComment(s)
    throw "createComment has no body yet"

export setAttribute(node, name, value)
    throw "setAttribute has no body yet"

export removeAttribute(node, name)
    throw "removeAttribute has no body yet"

export setProperty(node, name, value)
    throw "setProperty has no body yet"

// **`setChildren` writes the whole list and the two below it move one node**, and both spellings are
// here because they cost different things: replacing a list is what makes a re-render idempotent,
// and a browser records a mutation per child for it. `replaceChildren` takes the children as
// arguments, so the array is spread at the call.
export setChildren(node, kids)
    throw "setChildren has no body yet"

export setText(node, s)
    throw "setText has no body yet"

// **`before` of `null` appends**, which is `Node.insertBefore`'s own rule, and a node already in the
// page MOVES rather than being copied -- so it keeps its focus, its scroll position and whatever it
// was playing.
export insertBefore(parent, node, before)
    throw "insertBefore has no body yet"

// **The parent is named for the removal too**, where the host needs only the child: a reconciler
// holds both, and naming the parent is what turns "that node is somewhere else entirely" from a
// silent success into a sentence.
export removeChild(parent, node)
    throw "removeChild has no body yet"

// **The offset is in characters and the host counts UTF-16 units**, so the cut has to be converted
// before `splitText` is called or an offset written after an emoji lands inside one.
export splitText(node, at)
    throw "splitText has no body yet"

// **A no-op, kept so that nothing written against the built-in module has to change.** There is no
// handle table here and therefore nothing to give back; the element stays exactly where it is in the
// page, which is what `release` always did.
export release(node)
    throw "release has no body yet"

// -- finding a node, and reading one --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **These exist because hydration needs exactly them**: a program adopting markup a server rendered
// has to walk what is there and ask what it found. Everything above either makes a node or changes
// one.
//
// **`children` answers EVERY child node and not only the elements**, because a text node between two
// elements is a position: a reconciler counting children has to count what the browser counts, or it
// adopts the wrong node. `childNodes` is a host list rather than an array, so it is walked by its
// `length` and copied into a slate array here.

export byId(id)
    throw "byId has no body yet"

export query(selector)
    throw "query has no body yet"

export children(node)
    throw "children has no body yet"

// **Lower case, because that is what a program wrote** -- the host answers `DIV` for HTML -- and
// `null` for anything that is not an element, which is how a text node is told from one.
export tagName(node)
    throw "tagName has no body yet"

export nodeText(node)
    throw "nodeText has no body yet"

// **`true` for a bare attribute**, which is the reading `setAttribute` writes: it puts the empty
// string for `true`, so a program comparing what it would set against what is there gets the same
// value back. A name the node does not carry is `null`.
export attribute(node, name)
    throw "attribute has no body yet"

// `"element"`, `"text"`, `"comment"`, `"fragment"`, `"document"` or `"other"`, read off `nodeType`.
// **It tells a comment from a text node**, which `tagName` calls `null` alike -- a reconciler
// counting children read a comment as a piece of text and adopted the wrong node from there on.
export nodeKind(node)
    throw "nodeKind has no body yet"

// **What the node HOLDS, where `attribute` is what its markup said.** Only a value slate can hold
// comes back -- a string, a boolean or a number -- and anything else answers `null`, exactly as a
// name the node does not carry does. The marshalling already draws that line: a host value that is
// not a primitive crosses as an external, so this is a type test on what came back.
export property(node, name)
    throw "property has no body yet"

// **A reader and not a parser**: it answers the node's `outerHTML`, and for a node that has none --
// a text node, a comment -- the escaped text it says. Nothing in this module turns a string of
// markup into nodes.
export markup(node)
    throw "markup has no body yet"

// -- events -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A handler is given a RECORD and never the event**: `{ type, value, checked, key, mods, button,
// stop, prevent }`, eight fields built here from the host's event. A `MouseEvent` has no
// representation in slate, and handing one over as an external would put a value in a program's
// hands that it could not print, compare or store -- and would make every handler in every
// consumer read host properties instead of slate fields.
//
// `mods` is `{ meta, ctrl, shift, alt }` and `button` is an integer or `null` -- `0` left, `1`
// middle, `2` right. **They are here because a link cannot be written without them**: a framework
// that intercepts a click has to let a cmd-click, a ctrl-click, a shift-click and a middle click
// through to the browser, or it swallows the most ordinary thing anybody does to a link.
//
// `stop` and `prevent` are slate functions closing over the host event, which is the one place a
// foreign value is deliberately kept alive past the call that made it -- for as long as the handler
// runs and no longer.
//
// ## Taking a listener off again
//
// **`off` needs the same JavaScript function `on` handed over, and slate cannot name it.** A closure
// crosses outward as a fresh JavaScript function every time it crosses, so the value
// `removeEventListener` would be given is never the value `addEventListener` was given, and the
// listener would stay on the node for ever.
//
// **So the package keeps its own map, keyed by the slate closure itself.** slate compares a function
// by identity -- the rule `values.md` states for a set, a map, a promise and a function alike -- so
// the closure a consumer holds is a key that finds what was installed for it and finds nothing for
// any other closure. What the map holds is an `AbortController`, reached as
// `globalThis.AbortController`: `on` passes its signal to `addEventListener` and `off` calls
// `abort()`, which is the DOM's own way of removing a listener without naming the function again.
//
// **The controller has to come from the SAME realm as the node.** A signal built by node's own
// `AbortController` handed to a jsdom element is a foreign object to the interface that checks it,
// so a harness that installs a document has to install that document's `AbortController` beside it.
// A page has one realm and never meets this; a test harness has two.
//
// The map is a `Map` of node to a `Map` of event name to a `Map` of closure to controller, which is
// exactly the three-level table the built-in module keeps in its own runtime.
//
// **The outer level is what the language cannot do yet.** A `Map` keyed by a node compares its keys
// with `==`, which is right, and hashes them by the WRAPPER slate made at the crossing rather than
// by the host object behind it -- so two reads of one element are equal and land in two slots, and
// the map loses the key it was just given. Until that is fixed the outer level cannot be a `Map`
// keyed by the node.

export on(node, event, fn)
    throw "on has no body yet"

export off(node, event, fn)
    throw "off has no body yet"

// **What `on` reads, sent.** The argument is a string or a record carrying `type` and optionally
// `key`, `button`, `mods`, `bubbles` and `cancelable` -- the event record's own fields rather than
// the host's constructors. It answers whether nothing cancelled the event, which is all a sender can
// learn.
export dispatch(node, event)
    throw "dispatch has no body yet"

// A `MutationObserver`. The options are `children`, `attributes`, `text` and `subtree`, at least one
// of the first three; the answer is an object whose `disconnect` is the one thing that stops it.
//
// **A record carries no nodes**, which is the decision the event record already makes: a re-render
// makes hundreds of records. A record says `type` (`"children"`, `"attributes"` or `"text"`),
// `attribute` (the name, or `null`), and `added` and `removed` as counts.
export observe(node, options, fn)
    throw "observe has no body yet"

// An `EventSource`. A message is `{ data, lastEventId, type }` and an error record says only
// `closed`; the answer is an object whose `close` ends the subscription.
//
// **A `lastEventId` option is refused rather than ignored.** The host sends the last id it saw on
// its own reconnections and gives a program no way to set one on the first request, so taking the
// option and dropping it would be a program that believed it had resumed and had not.
export events(url, options)
    throw "events has no body yet"

export focus(node)
    throw "focus has no body yet"

export blur(node)
    throw "blur has no body yet"

// **The body answers `null` too.** A browser answers `<body>` for "nothing focused", so a program
// reading this has one value to check rather than two; one that wants to know what the body holds
// asks for it by name.
export activeElement()
    throw "activeElement has no body yet"

// -- where the page is, and where it has been ----------------------------------------------------
//
// **`location` is read off `window.location` and the four movements go through `window.history`.**
// A record is built here rather than the host object handed over, for the reason the event record is
// built: `{ href, protocol, host, hostname, port, path, query, hash }` is a value a program can
// keep, and `path` is the host's `pathname` under the name the rest of slate uses.
//
// **`query` and `hash` carry their punctuation** -- `"?a=1"` and `"#top"` -- which is what a program
// pasting one back into a url needs. An empty one is `""` and never `null`.
//
// **A push does not raise `onNavigate`.** The host raises `popstate` for a movement the user made
// and never for one the program made itself, so `onNavigate` is a `popstate` listener on the window
// and nothing else -- a router renders after its own push and waits to be told about everything
// else. Getting this wrong is how a router renders twice.
//
// **There is no state object, and that is a measurement rather than an omission**: `pushState`
// structured-clones what it is given and that strips the prototype, so a slate object put in comes
// back a plain object nothing in the language could read. The url is the whole of the state.

export location()
    throw "location has no body yet"

export pushPath(url)
    throw "pushPath has no body yet"

export replacePath(url)
    throw "replacePath has no body yet"

export back()
    throw "back has no body yet"

export forward()
    throw "forward has no body yet"

export onNavigate(fn)
    throw "onNavigate has no body yet"

// -- what the browser remembers -------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **The store answers results**, which is `readFileSync`'s channel and its reason: what comes back
// was written by somebody else -- another tab, an earlier visit, a user who cleared it. A key that
// is not there is `{ ok: true, value: null }` and not a failure; a browser told to keep no data, or
// one out of quota, is `{ ok: false, error }`. So every call here is wrapped in a `try`.
//
// **A value that is not a string is stored as slate prints it**, a store holding nothing else.

export stored(key)
    throw "stored has no body yet"

export store(key, v)
    throw "store has no body yet"

export unstore(key)
    throw "unstore has no body yet"

export storedKeys()
    throw "storedKeys has no body yet"

export clearStored()
    throw "clearStored has no body yet"

// -- what rides along with every request ----------------------------------------------------------
//
// **`localStorage` never leaves the browser and a cookie always does**, which is the whole reason a
// server-rendering framework reaches for one: a session id, a CSRF token, anything the NEXT request
// needs to already be carrying. All four read and write `document.cookie`, which is one string.
//
// **These answer plain values and never a result** -- reading `document.cookie` cannot fail the way
// a quota can, so a name that is not set is `null` rather than a fault.
//
// **The value is encoded and the options are not.** `setCookie` percent-encodes the value the same
// way `cookie` decodes it back -- a `;` or `=` inside it would otherwise end the pair or split it in
// the wrong place -- while `path`, `domain`, `expires`, `maxAge` and `sameSite` are written into the
// cookie string exactly as given, being the browser's syntax rather than a value a program
// round-trips.
//
// **A delete needs the SAME path and domain a set was given.** A cookie is identified by all three
// together, so a path-less `deleteCookie` after a `setCookie` with a path writes a second cookie
// rather than removing the first. That is `document.cookie`'s own trap, carried through rather than
// papered over.

export cookies()
    throw "cookies has no body yet"

export cookie(name)
    throw "cookie has no body yet"

export setCookie(name, v, options = {})
    throw "setCookie has no body yet"

export deleteCookie(name, options = {})
    throw "deleteCookie has no body yet"
